  {
    "episode_id": 85930,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85930.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 296,
    "turn_text": "What do you wish voters knew about you that they don't? Well, one, I mean, just my background is, you know, I'm a blue collar kid. My parents were working class. I worked minimum wage jobs to be able to get into school, do all that, put myself in a position to succeed. I ended up, did earn degrees from Yale and Harvard Law School, but I made the decision that in the post-9-11 world, you ought to serve, you ought to wear the uniform. So I volunteered, volunteered to serve in Iraq, alongside SEAL Team One and do all these other things. And I think it's just when you're running for president, I think people want to know, it's like, okay, well, why are you doing this? Is this about you or is it about us, the people? And I think my track record, kind of where I came from, my belief in the American dream and my you know, desire to restore it, a service to the country. I think they can look at it and say, you know what, this guy's in it for the right reasons. He wants to do something",
